{"name":"Arts and Crafts","hex":"#7d6549","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"arts and crafts","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["All the handicrafts based upon making decorative and useful things manually."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/arts_and_crafts"}],"entries":[{"title":"Arts and Crafts movement","blocks":[{"text":"The Arts and Crafts movement was an international trend in the decorative and fine arts that developed earliest and most fully in the British Isles and subsequently spread across the British Empire and to the rest of Europe and North America. Broadly, the beginning of the movement can be dated to the 1880s, with the term itself first used in 1887, and by about 1920 it can be considered as over in the UK, although the term continued in spurious use for various popular elements in design."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/William_Morris_design_for_Trellis_wallpaper_1862.jpg/330px-William_Morris_design_for_Trellis_wallpaper_1862.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arts_and_Crafts_movement","wikidata":"Q330369"},{"title":"Handicraft","blocks":[{"text":"A handicraft is a traditional main sector of craft making and applies to a wide range of creative and design activities that are related to making things with one's hands and skill, including work with textiles, moldable and rigid materials, paper, plant fibers, clay, etc. Usually, the term is applied to traditional techniques of creating items that are both practical and aesthetic. Handicraft industries are those that produce things with hands to meet the needs of the people in their locality without using machines."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Women_Making_Batik%2C_Ketelan_crop.jpg/330px-Women_Making_Batik%2C_Ketelan_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Handicraft","wikidata":"Q877729"},{"title":"The arts","blocks":[{"text":"The arts, or creative arts, are a vast range of human practices involving creative expression, storytelling, and cultural participation. The arts encompass diverse and plural modes of thought, deeds, and existence in an extensive range of media. Both a dynamic and characteristically constant feature of human life, the arts have developed into increasingly stylized and intricate forms. This is achieved through sustained and deliberate study, training, or theorizing within a particular tradition, across generations, and even between civilizations. The arts are a medium through which humans cultivate distinct social, cultural, and individual identities while transmitting values, impressions, judgements, ideas, visions, spiritual meanings, patterns of life, and experiences across time and space."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Gauguin%2C_Paul_-_Still_Life_with_Profile_of_Laval_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/330px-Gauguin%2C_Paul_-_Still_Life_with_Profile_of_Laval_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_arts","wikidata":"Q2018526"},{"title":"Craft","blocks":[{"text":"A craft or trade is a pastime or an occupation that requires particular skills and knowledge of skilled work. In a historical sense, particularly the Middle Ages and earlier, the term is usually applied to people occupied in small scale production of goods, or their maintenance, for example by tinkers. The traditional term craftsman is nowadays often replaced by artisan and by craftsperson."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Craft_fair_and_folk_festival_Bangladesh_2024_at_Sonargaon_museum_35.jpg/330px-Craft_fair_and_folk_festival_Bangladesh_2024_at_Sonargaon_museum_35.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Craft","wikidata":"Q2207288"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Kelmscott_Press_-_The_Nature_of_Gothic_by_John_Ruskin_%28first_page%29.jpg/500px-Kelmscott_Press_-_The_Nature_of_Gothic_by_John_Ruskin_%28first_page%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Kelmscott_Press_-_The_Nature_of_Gothic_by_John_Ruskin_%28first_page%29.jpg/500px-Kelmscott_Press_-_The_Nature_of_Gothic_by_John_Ruskin_%28first_page%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Nature of Gothic by John Ruskin, printed by William Morris at the Kelmscott Press in 1892 in his Golden Type inspired by the 15th-century printer Nicolas Jenson. This chapter from The Stones of Venice was a sort of manifesto for the Arts and Crafts movement.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKelmscott_Press_-_The_Nature_of_Gothic_by_John_Ruskin_(first_page).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/The_Grange%2C_Ramsgate_1.jpg/500px-The_Grange%2C_Ramsgate_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/The_Grange%2C_Ramsgate_1.jpg/1280px-The_Grange%2C_Ramsgate_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/The_Grange%2C_Ramsgate_1.jpg/500px-The_Grange%2C_Ramsgate_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pugin's house \"The Grange\" in Ramsgate, from 1843. Its simplified Gothic Revival style, adapted to domestic building, helped shape the architecture of the Arts and Crafts movement.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Grange%2C_Ramsgate_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/William_Morris_age_53.jpg/500px-William_Morris_age_53.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/William_Morris_age_53.jpg/1280px-William_Morris_age_53.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/William_Morris_age_53.jpg/500px-William_Morris_age_53.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"William Morris, a designer and thinker who was a key influence on the Arts and Crafts movement","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWilliam_Morris_age_53.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Philip_Webb%27s_Red_House_in_Upton.jpg/500px-Philip_Webb%27s_Red_House_in_Upton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Philip_Webb%27s_Red_House_in_Upton.jpg/1280px-Philip_Webb%27s_Red_House_in_Upton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Philip_Webb%27s_Red_House_in_Upton.jpg/500px-Philip_Webb%27s_Red_House_in_Upton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"William Morris's Red House in Bexleyheath, designed by Philip Webb and completed in 1860; one of the most significant buildings of the Arts and Crafts movement[30]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhilip_Webb's_Red_House_in_Upton.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Morris_and_Company_Weaving_at_Merton_Abbey.jpg/500px-Morris_and_Company_Weaving_at_Merton_Abbey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Morris_and_Company_Weaving_at_Merton_Abbey.jpg/1280px-Morris_and_Company_Weaving_at_Merton_Abbey.jpg?utm_source=en.wi